  "image_name": "1bfb6a283a67590f146b0e2b3784f21b.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates membrane fusion during viral entry and how host restriction factors can block it. On the left, a viral membrane approaches a cellular membrane, and viral glycoproteins insert a fusion peptide into the host membrane. This drives the two bilayers into a hemifusion state, where the outer leaflets merge first. The diagram then shows the usual progression through intermediate structures labeled as a stalk and diaphragm, followed by formation and enlargement of a fusion pore. Early and late pore states are depicted, implying successful opening of a channel that would allow viral contents to enter the cell.\n\nThe right side contrasts this with an inhibitory scenario involving 25HC and IFITM3 in the cellular membrane. These components appear to alter membrane properties, especially rigidity and curvature. Although hemifusion can still occur, the altered membrane environment prevents the transition to a full fusion pore. As a result, pore formation is blocked after hemifusion rather than proceeding to productive fusion. Overall, the figure emphasizes that viral fusion is a multistep membrane-remodeling process and that host factors can stop infection by interfering with the final pore-opening step.",
